00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:19.520
As we sink deeper into the fever dream that is this unusually divisive and fear filled season,

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.160
it's nice to have an antidotal feeling in the form of winter holidays.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.840
Throughout which, despite being co-opted by Hallmark commercialism, a sense of family, of coming together

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:38.100
predominates. My family ritual of this time of year is the annual broadcast of Frank Capra's It's a Wonderful Life

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:44.180
on Christmas Eve. What was once a postwar box office flop has become a cultural phenomenon,

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:49.420
recognized as one of the great cinematic masterpieces by one of our very best directors.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:55.320
Watching it, of course, it's tempting to brush off this film as overly sentimental an nostalgic, which it is.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:01.180
But I think we judge it like this at our own peril. Indeed, with It's a Wonderful Life, Capra interrogates

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.600
some of the key contradictions at the heart of American life to see whether or not they can be reconciled

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.160
or if, in fact, they don't exist at all. Contradictions between the individual and the community,

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.580
between adventure and domesticity and between success and an ordinary life.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:23.900
He stages these contradictions in the figure of George Bailey, a citizen of the small town of Bedford Falls

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:29.640
and the president of the local Building &amp; Loan. As the film begins, we don't know exactly what has happened

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.840
to George; only that he's in serious trouble and has reached the end of his rope and that he's about to be

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.760
aided by divine intervention in the form of a guardian angel seeking to earn his wings.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.200
We learn about George's life as his angle does in a series of flashbacks.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:48.020
"If you're going to help a man, you want to know something about him, don't you?"

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:52.180
George, we learn, has big dreams of leaving Bedford Falls, traveling the world

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.420
and becoming a metropolitan architect.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.520
"I'm shaking the dust of this crummy little town off my feet and I'm gonna see the world!"

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:04.060
But his selflessness, his drive to do the right thing, repeatedly comes in the way of these dreams.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:08.660
We also learn about Bedford Falls, a close knit community of mostly good people

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:14.740
leading simple lives, which is under the constant threat of being taken over by a ruthless business man

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.020
called Mr. Potter.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.660
The only thing standing in Potter's way is George Bailey, his father and their empathetic

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.500
Building &amp; Loan operation.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:28.440
The foil to Potter, the clear representation of corruption and evil in the film, is not George himself

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.300
but Mary Hatch, George's eventual wife, a compassionate young woman who is quite happy

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.540
to stay and live in Bedford Falls.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:41.240
It's important to note that both Potter and Mary show up in every flashback, representing George's desires

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:46.460
for fame and fortune on the one hand, and his drive to do the right thing for his community on the other.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:53.580
Of course, it's Mary's side that eventually wins out, but for a large majority of the film, George is not rewarded

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:59.320
for doing the right thing. He's punished. When young George rescues his brother from a freezing lake,

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:04.300
he loses the hearing in his left ear. When he warns his boss about a potentially disastrous accident

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:08.880
he gets beaten. When his father dies and he takes on his responsibilities at the Building &amp; Loan,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:14.080
he loses his chance for travel and college. When there's a run on the bank, he misses his own honeymoon.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:20.420
Every noble sacrifice for Bedford Falls makes Bedford Falls feel more like a trap.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:25.720
And instead of bringing him closer to the community he's helping, Capra shows him isolated from it.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:32.200
In this shot, after realizing that his dreams of travel are finally squashed for good, George stands isolated

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:36.960
against a train that he'll never get on before he walks slowly back into the crowd,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.660
putting a smile on again, like he always does.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.500
Despite this parade of personal wounds, George is able to remain resilient until the very end,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:51.980
when his scatterbrained uncle misplaces $8,000 and puts him in danger of going to jail for fraud.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:56.460
Things get worse and worse and George, believing himself worth more dead than alive,

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.060
contemplates suicide.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.100
It's at this point that his guardian angel Clarence intervenes.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:08.700
Clarence Oddbody, angle second class, shows George what Bedford Falls would have been like

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:15.480
had he never existed. What George sees is a nightmare. Bedford Falls is now Pottersville

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:21.780
and all the dreams he had of an exciting life of adventure and exoticism have returned as perverted

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:25.340
versions of themselves on the main drag of his former home.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:31.000
The moon that George promised to lasso for Mary, that she had made into a lovely needlepoint picture

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.680
is made ugly and sleazy in the form of the Blue Moon Cafe.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:39.140
Martini's Bar, a place where any break from decent behavior was not tolerated

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.180
has become a garish speakeasy where anything goes.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.360
Everything is turned into an opportunity for crass commercialism. Even George's home has become

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:54.760
a boarding house. And when George finally gets his old life back, he's rewarded with generosity

00:04:54.760 --> 00:05:00.680
from the community who bail him out in an unbelievably touching finale that ends with everyone,

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.420
including the cops who came to arrest him singing Auld Land Syne.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:13.460
"For auld lang syne, my dear, for auld lang- "

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:21.380
It's easy to get carried away in this wave of feeling. But it's important to remember what hasn't changed.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.500
And that's basically everything.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:29.320
George Bailey still has to live in his drafty, old house, scraping by to support his family,

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.640
his business, despite being bailed out by the town, remains at the brink of being swallowed

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:36.040
by Potter's relentless expansion.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:41.060
Indeed, Potter faces no consequences at all, despite being the main protagonist, depsite stealing money

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:45.460
from George Bailey, he's not vanquished. He's up $8,000.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:50.160
In some sense, Capra reconciles the contradictions I mentioned before.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:56.540
Success is not mutually exclusive to an ordinary life. Adventure can be found in a domestic setting.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:03.540
And yet, the most basic contradiction, that between the individual and the community, is left open ended.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:09.500
America has struggled with this contradiction since before its founding. How does a culture predicated

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:16.500
on individualism maintain public virtue? How does an economic system that incentivizes individual success

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:23.920
keep from exploiting communities? If you have the answer, tell us, because we're still, at the eve of 2016,

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:25.580
struggling to figure it out.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:32.060
I feel George's dilemma, as I'm sure many of you do. The world shines with the promise of adventure

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:37.200
and possibility, all that could be gained, experienced, learned, if only you were to commit yourself

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:43.440
to a radical individualism. And yet the responsibilities of family and of the community nag;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:49.920
They call us back, they ask us, sacrifice. In solution to this, everyone finds their own balance,

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:55.240
but you should keep in mind that it's these personal dilemmas played out in society at large

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:58.840
that ultimately draw the fault lines in our politics.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.920
In Frank Capra and George Bailey's time, following World War II, the idea of an American destiny,

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:11.780
of the American way still had weight and consensus. A string of disasters have weakened our resolve

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:18.720
in such a thing, have fractured our ability to imagine a consensus and into the void rushes the heirs

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:24.820
to Henry Potter. This is why the message of It's a Wonderful Life, a message of hope and perspective

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:32.200
is not so shallow as it seems. The contradictions in the film are never quite resolved, something beautifully

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.460
dramatized by the finial on George's stairs.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:44.740
There will always be an incompleteness to life, but hope is not naive. It's hardiness and resolve against

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:48.660
a situation that cannot give us assurances or guarantees.

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:54.300
The film shows us that a change of heart and perspective, far from being powerless nostalgia

00:07:54.300 --> 00:08:03.300
in the face of an unusually divisive and fear filled season can be a genuinely revolutionary attitude.

